From 09f8a4f9edab34db744113ae81d77727639ca83f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 22:15:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge, windowing/gbm: the GUI over Dolby Vision
 composited by the packer, from the GUI texture

On an Intel HD Graphics 610 (a user's diagnostic, profile 7 FEL at
fidelity step-down level 3, frames presented a frame ahead), the frames
drawn directly took 34 ms of GPU time of the 41.7 ms of a refresh; with
the player information open every frame was composed, and took 103 ms:
95 frames shown in 10 s, the sound stalled.

The composed way of the source transport made the picture that Kodi
draws over in a 3840x2160 RGBA32F texture (pass 3), CompositeGui()
blended the GUI into it (its fragments over the whole raster, a read and
a write of 32 bytes where the GUI covers), and pass 4 read it back: 64
bytes a pixel under the GUI, which the background of the player
information spreads over three quarters of the raster.

Now, for a frame presented a frame ahead (the GPUs that need it), in
YCbCr (affine decoding: profiles 7 and 8; profile 5 and the IPT tunnel
convert what is drawn over in pass 8, from pass 3), with Kodi's composite
into PQ in full range, pass 4 composites the GUI itself at each pixel
(PACK_GUI): the GUI texture sampled where the composite samples it, with
its own bilinear filter, the arithmetic of gles_gui_composite.frag where
fragment shaders have highp (the sRGB EOTF, BT.709 to BT.2020 in linear
light, the PQ signal of the GUI white, the coverage squared) and Kodi's
blend factors, and nothing is written into the RGBA32F picture. The tiles
that the GUI may reach (passes 6 and 7) are kept: elsewhere pass 4 takes
the codes of the transport as before. A frame that something else is
drawn into (PQ subtitles, a capture) makes the picture whole and gives
the composite back to Kodi (dvbridge_source_complete()). Such a frame
takes no part of the GUI cache, and its level 5 block takes every edge
with bars, as every frame presented a frame ahead already does.

Wherever the GUI covers nothing (its sample's alpha is 0, which the
composite discards) the pixel is the transport's, the same bytes by
construction. Under the GUI the same expressions as the composite and
the blend unit; measured with Mesa's zink over lavapipe against Kodi's
own composite shader, its quad, projection and blend factors: the same
transport bytes in every pixel, three pictures of a profile 7 FEL stream
at step-down levels 0 and 3, a GUI of 1920x1080 and of 3840x2160 that
covers 55 % of the raster; the IPT tunnel does not take it (the same
bytes as before); three mutations of the program are caught. A GPU's
blend unit may round apart from a shader by a code here and there under
a translucent GUI: not verified on a GPU.

advancedsettings.xml: <video><dvguiinplace>false</dvguiinplace></video>
composites the GUI as before. kodi.log says it once
("DVBridge: the GUI over the picture is composited by the packer of the
transport ..."), and the line of every 10 s counts the frames
("N frames with GUI elements composited by the packer, from the GUI
texture").

The idea of reading Kodi's menus in place, without a picture to
composite them into, comes from YBLOD (dangerouslaser), whose engine
does so in Vulkan; this is written for the passes of this bridge.
